package ferrum:plugin@1.0.0;

// CAPABILITY. Grant name: "status-item".
// Consent: "put an icon in the sidebar and show a reading when you click it".

/// A small persistent reading in the window's chrome, and what it says when
/// somebody asks.
///
/// **This is not a UI surface and must not become one.** The plugin supplies a
/// number and some label-and-value rows. The host decides where the icon sits,
/// how big it is, what the sheet looks like when it opens, in what typeface and
/// with what colours — and whether to show any of it at all. No geometry, no
/// colour, no widget, no handle to anything drawn.
///
/// The line to hold is between *content* and *layout*. A list of rows is
/// content: the host is free to render it as a table, a definition list, or
/// three lines of prose on a phone-sized window, and nothing in the guest
/// notices. The moment a guest can say where a row goes, how wide it is, or
/// what colour it is, this has become `cap-panels`, which is reserved and
/// deliberately absent.
///
/// It is deliberately narrower than `cap-panels` and is not a first instalment
/// of it. The vocabulary is fixed at what a glanceable reading needs — a
/// proportion, a few facts, a line of context — because a vocabulary that grows
/// one field at a time is how a constrained widget becomes an unconstrained one.
///
/// # The icon is not in this interface
///
/// A plugin's icon is declared in its manifest and shipped with the artifact,
/// not set through a function here. That is on purpose: an icon is the thing a
/// person clicks, so it is identity, and identity is reviewed at publish time
/// along with everything else in the manifest. A runtime `set-icon` would let a
/// plugin become something else after it was consented to.
interface status-item {
  use types.{command-id, host-error};

  /// Where the host is willing to put one.
  ///
  /// A closed enum rather than coordinates. Slots are the host's layout
  /// vocabulary; a plugin naming a pixel would break the moment the window
  /// changed, and a plugin naming *another plugin's* pixel is a fight the host
  /// would have to referee.
  enum slot {
    /// The activity rail, with the editor's own controls. Narrow: an icon,
    /// and a small mark over it if there is something to say.
    rail,
    /// The trailing end of the status bar, shared with the host's own
    /// readings, so the host may abbreviate or drop it under pressure.
    status-bar,
  }

  /// How much attention the reading deserves. The host maps these to its
  /// theme; a guest cannot name a colour, because a guest that could name a
  /// colour would name red and every plugin would be an emergency.
  enum tone {
    normal,
    warn,
    error,
  }

  /// The state of the icon itself.
  ///
  /// Everything here is advisory: the host truncates, abbreviates and drops as
  /// its own layout requires, and a rail may have room for the icon and
  /// nothing else.
  record reading {
    /// What this is, for a tooltip and for the sheet's heading. Short.
    label: string,

    /// A proportion in `[0, 1]`, which the host may draw as a ring, a bar or
    /// nothing at all. `none` is the right answer for a reading that is not a
    /// proportion.
    ///
    /// Clamped by the host rather than rejected. A plugin that computes 1.02
    /// from a quota that briefly overshoots should draw as full, not fail.
    fraction: option<f32>,

    /// One line for a tooltip: "37% used, resets in 54 min".
    summary: option<string>,

    tone: tone,
  }

  /// One fact in the sheet.
  ///
  /// A label, a value, and optionally a proportion the host may draw as a bar.
  /// Not a cell, not a column, not a widget — the host is free to lay a list of
  /// these out however its window wants, and a host too narrow for bars simply
  /// does not draw them.
  ///
  /// The proportion is here rather than only on `details` because the
  /// motivating sheet has two meters in it, not one: a session window and a
  /// weekly window, each with its own figure and its own reset. A sheet that
  /// could show one bar would make the second fact a sentence, which is a
  /// worse answer to the same question.
  record fact {
    label: string,
    value: string,
    /// `[0, 1]`. `none` is a fact that is not a proportion, which most are.
    ///
    /// Clamped by the host rather than rejected, like every other fraction in
    /// this interface.
    fraction: option<f32>,
  }

  /// What opens when somebody clicks the icon.
  ///
  /// Content only. The host draws it as a popover, a sheet or a dialog as its
  /// own design dictates, and a guest cannot tell which it chose.
  record details {
    /// The heading. Usually the plugin's own name.
    title: string,

    /// The headline proportion, drawn large. `none` shows the facts alone.
    fraction: option<f32>,

    /// The line under the heading: "resets in 54 min".
    caption: option<string>,

    /// The rows. The host shows as many as fit and may drop the rest, so put
    /// the ones that matter first.
    facts: list<fact>,

    /// A quieter line at the bottom: where the number came from, when it was
    /// last read. Worth using — a number with no provenance is a number
    /// somebody has to trust blindly.
    footnote: option<string>,
  }

  /// A live item, for as long as the handle is held.
  ///
  /// A resource because its lifetime *is* the handle's lifetime: dropping it
  /// removes the icon. A guest that traps, unwinds or is torn down cannot leave
  /// a stale reading in the chrome claiming a quota that was last measured an
  /// hour ago — which is worse than showing nothing, because a number on screen
  /// is read as a current number.
  resource item {
    /// Replace what the icon shows.
    ///
    /// Fire and forget: no result, so it cannot be awaited and cannot block a
    /// redraw. The host coalesces updates on its own schedule, and a plugin
    /// cannot tell how often it actually repainted — a plugin that could would
    /// tune itself into the frame loop.
    set: func(to: reading);

    /// Replace what opens when the icon is clicked.
    ///
    /// Set ahead of the click rather than computed during it. A guest asked to
    /// produce content *while* somebody is waiting for a popover is a guest
    /// whose budget overrun is a visible stall; this way the host already has
    /// something to draw and the click is never slower than a repaint.
    ///
    /// `none` makes the icon inert, which is the right state for a reading
    /// that has nothing more to say.
    describe: func(as-details: option<details>);

    /// Run a manifest-declared command from the sheet instead of, or as well
    /// as, showing it — "Sign in", "Refresh now".
    ///
    /// A `command-id` rather than a callback: the command is already declared,
    /// already namespaced by plugin identity and already visible in the
    /// palette, so a click grants nothing that was not granted at install.
    action: func(label: string, command: option<command-id>);
  }

  /// Claim a slot.
  ///
  /// Fails when the slot is full, when this plugin already holds as many as
  /// the host allows, or when the host has the chrome hidden. A free function
  /// rather than a constructor because all three of those are ordinary
  /// answers, and a constructor cannot say no.
  claim: func(at: slot) -> result<item, host-error>;
}
